# Grade 3: teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'3-1-1': dict(
  tryit=["Visit each station: creation, the prophets, Jesus, the Bible.", "Write what God shows you at each.", "Share one with the class."],
  saintStory="Jerome was born around the year 347 in what is now Croatia. He studied in Rome and was a brilliant student, and he loved the great Latin writers. But a dream made him think about what he loved most, and he decided to give his life to God’s word.\nHe went to live as a hermit in the desert, learned Hebrew, and then settled in Bethlehem, near the cave where Jesus was born. There he translated the Old Testament from Hebrew and the New Testament from Greek into Latin, the common language of the people. This translation, the Vulgate, was used by the Church for over a thousand years. Jerome had a hot temper and liked to argue. But he worked hard on the Bible for thirty-four years.\nHe wrote, “Ignorance of Scripture is ignorance of Christ.” Jerome shows us that the Bible is how God speaks to us, and it is worth our care.",
  script=dict(
    open="Whisper a message to one child and have it passed around the circle, until the last child says it aloud. Notice how it changed. Then say, “It is hard to pass a message accurately. But when God wants to tell us something, he does it perfectly. Let’s see how.”",
    teach=["Say: “God wants us to know him. A friend tells you about himself, and God does the same. We call this Revelation, which means ‘uncovering.’ How has God spoken to us? First, through creation: when we look at the stars, a mountain or a baby, we see that someone great made them. Psalm 19 says, ‘The heavens declare the glory of God.’”", "Say: “Second, God spoke through the prophets, such as Moses, Isaiah and Jeremiah, who gave God’s messages to the people. And third, and most fully, he spoke through his Son. The letter to the Hebrews says: ‘In many and various ways God spoke of old to our fathers by the prophets; but in these last days he has spoken to us by a Son.’ Jesus is God’s Word made flesh.”", "Say: “The Bible is God’s word written down. God inspired real human authors to write what he wanted, so the Bible has both God and people as its author. The Bible is like the Holy Spirit’s book. And the Church, guided by the Holy Spirit, helps us understand it correctly, which is why we read the Bible with the Church. When we hear Scripture at Mass, God is speaking to us.”"],
    ask=["Name three ways God speaks to us. (Creation, prophets, Jesus; also the Bible and Church.)", "Why is Jesus called the Word of God? (He is God’s fullest message to us, God himself in person.)", "Who helps us understand the Bible? (The Church, led by the Holy Spirit.)"],
    practice=["Set up four stations: Creation (a leaf, a shell or photos of stars), Prophets (a pictured scroll with a short quote from Isaiah 9:6), Jesus (a crucifix and a Gospel), and the Bible (a Bible open to Psalm 19).", "Give each child a station sheet with four prompts: “God shows me…”, “God told his people…”, “God spoke to us in…”, “God speaks today by…”.", "Rotate every five minutes; children write one sentence at each station.", "Return to the circle, and share one thing each child discovered."],
    close="Read Psalm 19:1 aloud: “The heavens declare the glory of God.” Say: “Thank you, God, for speaking to us.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read one Bible verse tonight with your family.”")),

'3-1-2': dict(
  saint="Anthony the Great: a young man who heard the Gospel at Mass and changed his life.",
  tryit=["Race to find the Bible verses.", "Tell your partner what each says.", "Choose your favorite."],
  saintStory="Anthony was born in Egypt around the year 251. His parents were wealthy Christians, but they died when he was about twenty, and he was left to care for his younger sister and a large property.\nOne Sunday at Mass he heard the Gospel being read, where Jesus said to the rich young man, “Go, sell what you have, and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven; and come, follow me.” Anthony felt as if these words were meant for him. He gave away his land, provided for his sister, and went out into the desert to pray.\nMany people followed him to the desert to learn how to live for God. He is called the father of monks. Anthony shows what can happen when we really listen to the Bible: one verse can change a life.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up the Bible and a small library book. Ask, “What is the difference between this book and a regular book? Is it long or short? Who wrote it?” Take guesses. “The Bible is not one book, but a whole library of books.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Bible has 73 books. There are 46 in the Old Testament, and 27 in the New Testament. Some are history, some are poetry and songs, like the Psalms, some are wise sayings, some are letters, some are the words of prophets. The Old Testament tells the story of God’s people before Jesus, and how God prepared the world for him. The New Testament tells about Jesus and the first Church.”", "Say: “The heart of the Bible is the four Gospels: Matthew, Mark, Luke and John. ‘Gospel’ means ‘good news.’ They tell the story of Jesus’ life, death and Resurrection. We stand when the Gospel is read at Mass, because it is Jesus’ own words.”", "Read 2 Timothy 3:16: “All Scripture is inspired by God and is useful for teaching.” Say: “That means God is the author. We use a table of contents to find books, and chapters and verses to find passages. ‘John 3:16’ means the Gospel of John, chapter 3, verse 16.”"],
    ask=["How many books are in the Bible? (73.)", "What are the two parts called? (Old Testament and New Testament.)", "What does ‘Gospel’ mean? (Good news.)"],
    practice=["Give pairs a Bible and a list of eight references: Genesis 1:1, Psalm 23:1, Isaiah 9:6, Matthew 5:3, Luke 2:11, John 3:16, Acts 2:1, Romans 8:28.", "Show how to use the table of contents, then say, “Go!”", "Pairs write the book, the testament it is in, and what the verse says.", "Gather and share. Which verse is your favorite? Why?"],
    close="Read John 3:16 together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Find a Bible at home and look for your favorite verse.”")),

'3-1-3': dict(
  saint="King David: a shepherd boy who became king and received a great promise.",
  tryit=["Make a timeline of God’s covenants.", "Draw a symbol for each.", "Say what each covenant promised."],
  saintStory="David was the youngest son of a man named Jesse in Bethlehem. While his big brothers went to war, David looked after sheep and played the harp. God chose him, through the prophet Samuel, to be the next king of Israel, even though he was the youngest.\nHe is best known for beating the giant Goliath with a sling and a stone, trusting in God. David became a great king. He wrote many psalms, songs of praise and trust. He also did wrong, but when the prophet Nathan told him about it, David was truly sorry and wrote Psalm 51, “Create in me a clean heart, O God.”\nGod made a covenant with David, promising that one of his descendants would rule forever. That descendant is Jesus. David shows how God keeps his promises even when we fail.",
  script=dict(
    open="Have two children shake hands and say a promise to each other: “I promise to share my snack.” Ask, “What makes a promise special? What if you break it?” Say, “Now think of God making a promise. It would be the biggest promise.”",
    teach=["Say: “A covenant is a sacred promise between God and people. It is more than a contract. It makes people into God’s family. God made a series of covenants in the Old Testament, each one bigger than the one before. With Noah, God promised never to flood the earth again, and put the rainbow in the sky. With Abraham, he promised a great family and a land, and a blessing for all nations. With Moses, he gave the Law and made Israel his people. With David, he promised a king whose kingdom would last forever.”", "Say: “Each time, people broke the covenant. But God did not give up. The prophet Jeremiah said that God would make a new covenant, with a law written on our hearts. Jesus fulfilled it. At the Last Supper he took the cup and said, ‘This cup that is poured out for you is the new covenant in my blood.’ (Luke 22:20) At Mass, we celebrate that covenant. Jesus’ sacrifice makes us God’s family.”", "Say: “God keeps his promises. All the covenants lead to Jesus. And God invites us to keep our part: to love him and follow him. When we are baptized, we enter the new covenant.”"],
    ask=["What is a covenant? (A sacred promise that makes people into a family.)", "Who were the people God made covenants with? (Noah, Abraham, Moses, David.)", "How does Jesus make a new covenant? (By giving his Body and Blood.)"],
    practice=["Give each child a long strip of paper.", "Mark five spots and label them: Noah (rainbow), Abraham (stars), Moses (tablets), David (crown), Jesus (cup and bread).", "Under each, write the covenant’s promise in a sentence.", "Take turns presenting the timeline to a partner."],
    close="Pray: “God, thank you for keeping your promises.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Tell your family about God’s covenants.”")),

'3-2-1': dict(
  saint="Polycarp: a bishop who would not put anyone or anything before God.",
  tryit=["Build a “first place” stand for God.", "List the things that can crowd God out.", "Choose how to keep God first."],
  saintStory="Polycarp was bishop of the city of Smyrna (now in Turkey) around the year 155. As a young man he had learned about Jesus from Saint John the apostle, so he knew someone who knew Jesus.\nWhen he was eighty-six years old, the Roman authorities arrested him. They told him that if he would curse Christ and say “Caesar is Lord,” they would let him go. Polycarp answered, “Eighty-six years I have served him, and he has done me no wrong. How can I blaspheme my King who saved me?” He was put to death for his faith.\nThe Christians of Smyrna wrote a letter about him. Polycarp lived the first commandment: no one and nothing is more important than God. He also shows that old and young can be brave.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a toy on a pedestal, such as a cup, and say, “This is first place. Who is the most important person in the world to you? Who would be in first place in your life? Let’s find out where God is.”",
    teach=["Read Exodus 20:1-11 slowly. Say: “Notice how the commandments begin: ‘I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of Egypt, out of slavery.’ God first reminds the people that he loves them and has saved them. The commandments are not rules to hold us back. They are a way of loving God back. They are like the rules for a friendship.”", "Say: “Commandment 1: ‘You shall have no other gods before me.’ We worship God alone. A false god is anything that we put before God, like money, a game, popularity or our own wishes. Commandment 2: ‘You shall not take the name of the Lord your God in vain.’ God’s name is holy. We say it with love, not casually and not in anger. Jesus’ name is the same: it is a prayer. Commandment 3: ‘Remember the Sabbath day, to keep it holy.’ For Christians, Sunday is the Lord’s Day, the day of Jesus’ Resurrection. We go to Mass, rest, and spend time with family.”", "Say: “God does not need our worship. We need it. We are made to love him. Keeping God first makes everything else fall into place. It is like putting the biggest rocks into a jar first: everything else fits around them.”"],
    ask=["What does the first commandment ask? (Worship God alone, keep him first.)", "What are some things that could crowd God out? (Screens, sports, stuff, being busy.)", "Why do we keep Sunday holy? (It is the Lord’s Day, and we need time with God.)"],
    practice=["Give each child modeling clay. Say: “Make a little stand with a number 1 on top.”", "On an index card, list three things that could take God’s first place (video games, being popular, sports, sleep-in on Sunday).", "Beside each, write one way to keep God first (“pray before the game,” “go to Mass first”).", "Put the stand and card together and take them home as a reminder."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, you are first. Help me love you above all things.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Put your clay stand where you will see it.”")),

'3-2-2': dict(
  saint="Elizabeth Ann Seton: a wife, mother, widow and founder of schools who loved her neighbor.",
  tryit=["Act out a commandment in your own words.", "Guess which one the other team is acting.", "Say how it shows love."],
  saintStory="Elizabeth Bayley was born in New York in 1774, and she grew up in the Episcopal Church. She married William Seton, a wealthy merchant, and they had five children. Then William’s business failed, and he became very sick. The family sailed to Italy hoping to cure him, but he died there.\nIn Italy Elizabeth saw how Catholics prayed, and she came to believe in the Eucharist. Back in America she became Catholic, which cost her many friends. She had to support her children, so she opened a school for girls in Baltimore, and then started a community of sisters. She started the first Catholic girls’ school in the United States.\nElizabeth Ann Seton cared for her children and her pupils, for the poor and for the sick. She lived out the commandments to love our neighbor. She was the first native-born American saint.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a sign with the words, “Love God. Love your neighbor.” Ask, “Which is first? Which one is second? Are they connected? Jesus said they are the two most important commandments, and the other seven commandments teach us how to do the second.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Ten Commandments are on two tablets. The first three are about loving God. Commandments 4 to 10 are about loving our neighbor. Commandment 4: ‘Honor your father and mother.’ We respect and obey our parents, and care for them when they are old. Commandment 5: ‘You shall not kill.’ Life is a gift from God, so we never harm anyone. Commandment 6 and 9: faithfulness and purity. God made our bodies good, and we treat them, and others’ bodies, with respect.”", "Say: “Commandment 7: ‘You shall not steal.’ We do not take what is not ours. We share what we have. Commandment 8: ‘You shall not bear false witness.’ We tell the truth and we don’t gossip. Commandment 10: ‘You shall not covet.’ We do not envy what other people have. We are grateful for what God has given us.”", "Say: “Jesus summed up the commandments in one word: love. He said, ‘You shall love your neighbor as yourself.’ (Matthew 22:39) And he said, ‘If you love me, you will keep my commandments.’ The commandments show us what love looks like.”"],
    ask=["Which commandments are about our neighbor? (Four through ten.)", "How does ‘Do not steal’ show love? (We respect what belongs to others.)", "What one word sums up the commandments? (Love.)"],
    practice=["Write commandments 4 to 10 on cards in kid language: “Obey and honor,” “Do not hurt,” “Be faithful and pure,” “Do not take what is not yours,” “Do not lie,” “Be pure of heart,” “Do not envy.”", "Split into two teams. A child from each team draws a card and acts it out silently, without words.", "The team guesses the commandment, and then tells one way it shows love.", "Switch actors."],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, teach us to love our neighbor.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Show love to someone using one of the commandments.”")),

'3-2-3': dict(
  saint="Thomas More: a lawyer who would not go against his conscience.",
  tryit=["Work through the three situations.", "Ask: Is it kind? True? Would Jesus do it?", "Say what you would do."],
  saintStory="Thomas More was born in London in 1478. He became a lawyer and was famous for his wit and wisdom. He married and had four children and spent time with them every day. He also became an adviser to King Henry VIII and was made Lord Chancellor of England, the highest position in the land.\nWhen King Henry decided to break from the Pope and make himself head of the Church in England, he demanded that everyone take an oath agreeing with this. Thomas could not do it in good conscience. He remained silent, but he would not take the oath. He was put in prison in the Tower of London for over a year, and then executed in 1535.\nHis last words were reported to be that he died “the king’s good servant, but God’s first.” Thomas More teaches us to follow our conscience, formed by the truth.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a compass and say, “What does a compass do? It points north, no matter where you are. Do we have anything like that inside us? There is something that points us toward what is good.” Let children guess before naming it.",
    teach=["Say: “It is called conscience. Conscience is the voice in our heart that helps us know right from wrong. It tells us, ‘Do this. Avoid that.’ When we do something good, we feel peace. When we do something wrong, we feel uneasy. God put this voice in our hearts. Romans 2:15 says God’s law is written on our hearts.”", "Say: “But conscience, like a compass, can be off if it is not looked after. Our conscience needs to be trained. We train it by praying, learning the Bible and the Ten Commandments, listening to the Church, and asking advice from wise people, like parents and teachers. A conscience can be wrong if we don’t know the truth.”", "Say: “Two rules: First, we always follow our conscience. It is wrong to do what we think is wrong. Second, we always work to form it well, by learning what is true and good. And if we are not sure, we ask someone who knows. If we sin, we can go to Confession.”"],
    ask=["What is conscience? (The inner voice that helps us know right from wrong.)", "How do we train our conscience? (Prayer, the Bible, the Church, good advice.)", "What do we do if we aren’t sure? (Ask a wise person.)"],
    practice=["Present three situations one at a time: a cashier gives you extra change; a friend asks you to cheat on a test; a classmate is being teased.", "After each, give the children a minute to think. Pair them to discuss using three questions: “Is it kind? Is it true? What would Jesus do?”", "Take answers. Talk about what makes it hard.", "Pray the “Come, Holy Spirit” prayer for help in making choices."],
    close="Pray: “Come, Holy Spirit, fill the hearts of your faithful.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “When you must choose this week, ask the three questions.”")),

'3-3-1': dict(
  saint="Pier Giorgio Frassati: a joyful young man who lived the Beatitudes in the mountains and among the poor.",
  tryit=["Pick one Beatitude.", "Say it in your own words.", "Draw someone living it."],
  saintStory="Pier Giorgio Frassati was born in Turin, Italy, in 1901, to a wealthy family. His father owned a newspaper. Pier Giorgio had everything he needed, but he was not interested in showing off. He loved the mountains, and he went hiking and climbing with his friends, praying the Rosary on the way. His motto was “Verso l’alto,” or “To the heights.”\nHe also loved the poor. He visited poor families in the slums of Turin, bringing food and helping them, and often gave away his own bus fare and had to run home. He did this quietly, and his family did not know how much until his funeral, when huge crowds of poor people came.\nPier Giorgio died of polio at twenty-four, in 1925. He was named a saint in 2025. He lived the Beatitudes: merciful, pure of heart, and a peacemaker.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask the children, “If you could have anything to make you really happy, what would it be?” Write the answers on the board. “Now listen to what Jesus said would make us happy. It might surprise you.”",
    teach=["Read Matthew 5:3-12 slowly. Say: “Jesus sat on a mountain and taught his disciples. He gave eight promises of happiness. The word Beatitude comes from the Latin for ‘blessed.’ Each begins ‘Blessed are…’ and ends with a promise.”", "Go through them in kid words: “Blessed are the poor in spirit: those who know they need God. Blessed are those who mourn: God comforts them. Blessed are the meek: the gentle will inherit the earth. Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness: they will be satisfied. Blessed are the merciful: they will receive mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart: they will see God. Blessed are the peacemakers: they will be called children of God. Blessed are those who are persecuted for doing right: the kingdom of heaven is theirs.”", "Say: “These surprise us. The world says: be rich, be strong, get even. Jesus says: be humble, be gentle, forgive. The Beatitudes are a picture of Jesus himself, and the way of a disciple. And they lead to heaven. Jesus lived every one of them.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘blessed’ mean? (Happy, favored by God.)", "Which Beatitude is the hardest for you? Why? (Take answers.)", "How are the Beatitudes different from what the world says? (They reverse it: humble, gentle, merciful.)"],
    practice=["Assign each pair one Beatitude. Have them restate it in kid words.", "Each pair draws a scene of someone living it, such as a child comforting a friend who is sad.", "Write the Beatitude and a caption.", "Compile the pages into a class book, and read it aloud."],
    close="Read the Beatitudes one more time with a reader for each. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Choose one Beatitude to live this week.”")),

'3-3-2': dict(
  saint="Vincent de Paul: a priest whose faith, hope and love turned into action for the poor.",
  tryit=["Write an action for faith, hope and love.", "Add it to the chain.", "Choose one to do this week."],
  saintStory="Vincent was born in France in 1581, and grew up on a farm. He became a priest, and at first he wanted a comfortable life. But as a priest he saw how hard life was for the poor: hungry families, sick people with no care, and prisoners chained to the oars of galleys.\nVincent changed his heart. He collected food and money for the poor. He started groups of volunteers to visit the sick, and with Louise de Marillac, began the Daughters of Charity, sisters who served the poor in the streets. He founded priests to teach and serve the poor in the countryside. He is said to have told his helpers that the poor are our “lords and masters.”\nVincent shows faith in action: trusting that Christ is in the poor, hoping that things can change, and loving through deeds.",
  script=dict(
    open="Give each child a piece of paper with three words: faith, hope, love. Ask, “Which one is easiest for you? Which is the hardest?” Let them mark it and share with a partner.",
    teach=["Say: “At your Baptism, God gave you three special gifts called theological virtues, because they come from God and lead to God. They are faith, hope and love. Faith means believing in God and everything he has told us, even when we cannot see. Hope means trusting God’s promises, especially that he will bring us to heaven. Love, also called charity, means loving God above all, and loving others as God loves us.”", "Read 1 Corinthians 13:13: “So faith, hope and love abide, these three; but the greatest of these is love.” Say: “Paul says love is the greatest. That is because in heaven, we won’t need faith (we will see God), and we won’t need hope (we will have it), but love goes on forever. God is love.”", "Say: “These virtues are like muscles that grow when we use them. We practice faith when we pray and trust God. We practice hope when we do not give up. We practice love when we help someone, forgive and share. God gives us the gifts, and we say yes by acting.”"],
    ask=["What are the three theological virtues? (Faith, hope and love.)", "Which is the greatest? (Love.)", "How can we practice hope? (Trust God, don’t give up.)"],
    practice=["Cut paper strips in three colors, one for each virtue.", "Each child writes one action on each color: faith (“pray before a test”), hope (“keep trying when it’s hard”), love (“include someone who is alone”).", "Make links and join them in a class chain, color by color.", "Hang the chain and choose one link to try this week."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, increase my faith, my hope and my love.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Choose one link from the chain and do it.”")),

'3-3-3': dict(
  saint="Louis IX of France: a king who tried to be fair, brave and self-controlled.",
  tryit=["Go to each of the four stations.", "Solve the problem using the virtue.", "Say how it takes courage or self-control."],
  saintStory="Louis IX was born in 1214, and became king of France at twelve. His mother, Blanche of Castile, ruled for him until he was older, and she taught him that she would rather see him dead than commit a mortal sin.\nLouis tried to rule justly. He is remembered for sitting under an oak tree at Vincennes, listening to anyone who had a complaint, rich or poor. He set up courts to make justice fair, and ended unfair practices. He fasted, prayed the Hours, and gave to the poor. He fed beggars at his own table and washed the feet of the poor. He also went on two Crusades, was captured once, and died of illness in Tunis in 1270.\nLouis shows the four cardinal virtues: wisdom in choosing, justice in treating others fairly, courage in danger and self-control in living simply.",
  script=dict(
    open="Open a door and let it swing on its hinges. Say, “What does a hinge do? Without it, the door is useless. There are four ‘hinge’ virtues, too, because the rest of a good life swings on them.”",
    teach=["Say: “The word ‘cardinal’ comes from the Latin word for ‘hinge.’ The four cardinal virtues are the hinges of a good life. They are habits, things we get good at by practicing, and God helps us. The first is prudence. Prudence means choosing wisely. Before we act, we think about what is good and the best way to do it. Wisdom 8:7 says wisdom teaches temperance, prudence, justice and fortitude.”", "Say: “The second is justice. Justice means giving everyone what they deserve: fairness, respect, and what is owed. We share, we tell the truth, we keep promises. The third is fortitude, which means courage. It is the strength to do what is right even when it is hard or scary. The fourth is temperance, which means self-control. We use good things in the right amount. We don’t eat too much, watch too much or want everything.”", "Say: “Virtues are habits, like practicing a sport. The more we practice, the easier it gets. We build them by choosing good again and again. And with God’s grace they become part of us. We pray: ‘God, help me be wise, fair, brave and self-controlled.’”"],
    ask=["What are the four cardinal virtues? (Prudence, justice, fortitude, temperance.)", "Which one helps you resist too much screen time? (Temperance.)", "When is it hard to be brave? (Take answers: standing up for someone, telling the truth.)"],
    practice=["Set up four stations, one per virtue, each with a situation card. For example: Prudence: you must decide what to do after school; Justice: two children want the same toy; Fortitude: a younger child is being teased; Temperance: you have a plate of cookies.", "Groups visit each station for four minutes, discuss and decide what to do, and write or act it.", "After the rotation, discuss which was the hardest.", "Close with a one-sentence goal for each virtue."],
    close="Pray: “God, make me wise, fair, brave and self-controlled.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Practice one virtue this week.”")),

'3-4-1': dict(
  tryit=["Make a two-column poster.", "On one side: Jesus is human.", "On the other: Jesus is God."],
  saintStory="Athanasius was born around the year 296 in Alexandria, Egypt. As a young deacon he attended the Council of Nicaea in 325, where bishops from all over the Church met to answer a question: Is Jesus truly God? A priest named Arius said that Jesus was a creature, made by God, not equal with the Father. The Council said no: Jesus is truly God, “of one substance” with the Father.\nAthanasius spent the rest of his life defending this truth. When he became bishop of Alexandria, powerful people who believed Arius was right had him sent away. He was exiled five times, and spent about seventeen years away from his people, but he did not give up. People said it was “Athanasius against the world.”\nHe wrote a book called On the Incarnation, which says that God became a human being so that we could share in God’s life. Athanasius shows how much it matters that Jesus is both God and man.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a picture of a baby, and then a picture of a king on a throne. Ask, “Can one person be both? That’s the amazing news about Jesus.”",
    teach=["Read John 1:1,14: “In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God… And the Word became flesh and dwelt among us.” Say: “The Word is God’s Son, who always existed with the Father. At a point in time, he became a human being, in the womb of Mary. This is called the Incarnation: ‘in the flesh.’ Christmas celebrates it.”", "Say: “Jesus is true God and true man. He is fully God. He is the second Person of the Trinity. He has no beginning. He has the power to heal and forgive sins. And he is fully human. He had a body. He was born, grew tired, was hungry, got angry, cried at his friend’s grave, and died. He had a human mind and heart. He is exactly like us, except without sin.”", "Say: “Why did God become a man? To save us, to show us how much the Father loves us, and to show us how to live. Only someone who is both God and man can bring us back to God. A bridge needs to touch both sides of the river. Jesus is the bridge between God and us.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘Incarnation’ mean? (God the Son became flesh, a human being.)", "Give an example showing that Jesus is human. (He was hungry, tired, cried.)", "Give an example showing he is God. (He forgave sins, calmed the storm, rose from the dead.)"],
    practice=["Give each child a sheet of paper divided into two columns.", "Head them “Jesus is human” and “Jesus is God.” Brainstorm: for human, hungry, tired, wept, had a mother; for God, forgave sins, raised the dead, calmed the storm.", "Add Gospel references, such as Luke 8:24 (asleep in the boat) and Mark 4:39 (calms the storm).", "Draw a symbol at the top: a manger and a crown."],
    close="Say: “Jesus, you are true God and true man. We praise you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Tell someone why Jesus became a man.”")),

'3-4-2': dict(
  saint="Luke the Evangelist: a physician who wrote about Jesus’ healings.",
  tryit=["Sort the miracle cards.", "Say what each one shows about Jesus.", "Choose one to retell."],
  saintStory="Luke was a Greek doctor, and a friend and companion of Saint Paul. He was not one of the twelve apostles, and he never met Jesus during his life on earth. But he carefully collected the stories of those who did, as he says at the start of his Gospel: “I decided to write an orderly account for you.”\nLuke wrote the Gospel of Luke and the Acts of the Apostles. His Gospel has stories that are not found in the others: the Annunciation, the shepherds, the Good Samaritan, the Prodigal Son. He pays attention to the poor, women, and sinners, and to Jesus’ mercy. He had a doctor’s eye for the details of healing.\nTradition says that Luke was a painter, too, and that he painted a picture of Mary. He is the patron of doctors and artists. He shows us Jesus the healer.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “Imagine you are in a boat on a lake, and a huge storm comes. The waves are crashing in. The boat is filling with water. And Jesus is asleep in the back. What would you do?” Let children respond. Then read what the disciples did.",
    teach=["Read Mark 4:35-41 aloud. Say: “The disciples were afraid and woke him up: ‘Teacher, do you not care that we are perishing?’ Jesus stood up, and said to the wind, ‘Peace! Be still!’ And the wind stopped, and it was completely calm. Then he asked, ‘Why are you afraid? Have you no faith?’ And they asked each other, ‘Who is this, that even the wind and sea obey him?’”", "Say: “Jesus did many miracles. He healed the sick, made the blind see, fed five thousand people, and raised the dead. In Luke 8:40-56 he raises the daughter of Jairus, saying, ‘Little girl, arise!’ But miracles are not magic tricks. They are signs. A sign points to something. The miracles of Jesus point to who he is: the Son of God with power over nature, sickness and death. They show that God’s kingdom has come.”", "Say: “The miracles also show Jesus’ compassion. He healed because he loved people. And they call us to faith. Many people believed in Jesus because of his miracles. Jesus still works through the sacraments, such as the Anointing of the Sick, and in answer to prayer.”"],
    ask=["What is a sign? (Something that points to something else.)", "What did the calming of the storm show about Jesus? (He has power over nature, he is God.)", "Why did Jesus work miracles? (Compassion, to show who he is, to call us to faith.)"],
    practice=["Prepare cards of Gospel miracles: calming the storm, feeding five thousand, healing the blind man, raising Jairus’s daughter, walking on water, the wedding at Cana.", "Prepare two headings: “A sign about who Jesus is” and “Magic trick.” Sort the cards (all go under signs!).", "For each, write: “This shows Jesus is…”", "Choose one miracle and retell it to a partner."],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, we believe in you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read the story of a miracle with your family.”")),

'3-4-3': dict(
  saint="Joseph of Arimathea: a disciple who buried Jesus with honor.",
  tryit=["Fold your paper in three.", "Draw Friday, Saturday and Sunday.", "Tell the story to a partner."],
  saintStory="Joseph came from the town of Arimathea and was a rich man and a member of the Jewish council. The Gospels say that he was a good and righteous man who was waiting for the kingdom of God, and a secret disciple of Jesus, because he was afraid of what others would say.\nOn Friday afternoon, after Jesus had died, most of the disciples were hiding. But Joseph bravely went to Pilate, the Roman governor, and asked for Jesus’ body. With Nicodemus, another man who had come to Jesus at night, he took the body down from the cross, wrapped it in clean linen with spices, and laid it in his own new tomb, which was cut in the rock. Then they rolled a stone across the entrance.\nJoseph’s courage showed his love. No one was willing, and he stepped up. He shows what to do when it is hard to stand up for Jesus.",
  script=dict(
    open="Have the room very quiet. Say, “I am going to tell you the most important three days in the history of the world. They happened a very long time ago. Friday. Saturday. Sunday.”",
    teach=["Read Luke 23:33-46 in pieces. Say: “On Friday, Jesus was crucified. He was nailed to a cross between two criminals. He prayed, ‘Father, forgive them, for they know not what they do.’ He told the good thief, ‘Today you will be with me in paradise.’ At about three o’clock he cried out, ‘Father, into your hands I commit my spirit.’ And he died. It was the darkest day. Jesus did this out of love, to forgive our sins.”", "Say: “On Saturday his body lay in the tomb. His friends were sad and afraid, and the Sabbath was a day of rest. It was quiet. It seemed that all was lost.”", "Read Luke 24:1-12. Say: “Then, on Sunday morning, early, some women went to the tomb with spices. The stone had been rolled away. The tomb was empty! Two men in shining clothes said, ‘Why do you seek the living among the dead? He is not here, but has risen.’ Jesus rose from the dead on the third day! The Resurrection shows that Jesus is God, that death is not the end, and that heaven is open to us. Easter is the greatest feast of the year.”"],
    ask=["Why did Jesus die on the cross? (To forgive our sins and save us.)", "What happened on the third day? (He rose from the dead.)", "What does the Resurrection prove? (Jesus is God, he defeated death, and heaven is open.)"],
    practice=["Give each child a paper to fold into three columns.", "Label them: Friday: The Cross; Saturday: The Tomb; Sunday: The Empty Tomb.", "In each, draw a scene and write a sentence.", "Partner up and retell the three days. End by saying together: “He is risen!”"],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, you died for us and rose for us. Thank you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Tell your family the story of the three days.”")),

'3-5-1': dict(
  saint="Barnabas: a generous man whose name means “son of encouragement.”",
  tryit=["Write a gift you need from the Spirit on a flame.", "Add it to the wall.", "Pray for each other."],
  saintStory="Barnabas was a Jewish man from the island of Cyprus. His real name was Joseph, but the apostles called him Barnabas, which means “son of encouragement,” because of how he cheered people up.\nAfter Pentecost the first Christians shared everything they had. Barnabas had a field, sold it, and put the money at the apostles’ feet. When Saul, the man who had once persecuted Christians, came to Jerusalem, everyone was afraid of him. Barnabas welcomed him and introduced him to the apostles. Later, Barnabas and Saul, who became Paul, went on a mission trip together.\nBarnabas shows what the Holy Spirit does: he fills us with generosity, courage and the desire to encourage others.",
  script=dict(
    open="Put a candle out of reach, and a lighter or an electric candle. Say, “A candle is just wax and a wick until it is lit. The disciples were like unlit candles until Pentecost. Let’s see what happened.”",
    teach=["Tell Acts 2:1-4 with energy: “Fifty days after Easter, the disciples were gathered in a room in Jerusalem, afraid. Mary was with them. Suddenly there was a sound from heaven like a mighty wind. Something like tongues of fire appeared and rested on each of them. They were filled with the Holy Spirit, and began to speak in other languages.”", "Say: “Crowds from all over the world were in Jerusalem for the feast, and each one heard the apostles speaking in his own language. Peter stood up and preached to them boldly. He said that Jesus, who was crucified, God had made Lord and Christ. About three thousand people were baptized that day. This is the birthday of the Church.”", "Say: “The same Holy Spirit is given to us. We receive him in Baptism, and in a special way in Confirmation. The Spirit gives us gifts: wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, piety, and fear of the Lord. And he gives fruits, such as love, joy, peace and patience. The Spirit makes us brave to follow Jesus, helps us pray, and makes us one in the Church.”"],
    ask=["What happened at Pentecost? (The Holy Spirit came, like wind and fire.)", "What changed in the apostles? (They were brave and spoke about Jesus.)", "When do we receive the Holy Spirit? (Baptism and Confirmation.)"],
    practice=["Cut flame shapes from red, orange and yellow paper.", "Have each child write on a flame one gift they need from the Holy Spirit, such as courage, kindness, wisdom, patience.", "Tape the flames to a big paper cross, a dove, or a wall, as a class “Pentecost fire.”", "Stand in a circle and pray: “Come, Holy Spirit” after each child reads their gift."],
    close="Pray together the “Come, Holy Spirit” prayer. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask the Holy Spirit for your gift every morning.”")),

'3-5-2': dict(
  saint="Paul: the man who persecuted the Church and then became its greatest missionary.",
  tryit=["Put the four-piece church puzzle together.", "Write one word on each piece: One, Holy, Catholic, Apostolic.", "Explain each mark to a partner."],
  saintStory="Saul was a young Jewish man from the city of Tarsus. He was a strict student of the law, and he thought that the followers of Jesus were wrong. He was present at the death of Stephen, the first martyr, and he went from house to house arresting Christians.\nOn his way to the city of Damascus, a bright light flashed from heaven, and Saul fell to the ground. A voice said, “Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?” “Who are you, Lord?” “I am Jesus, whom you are persecuting.” Saul was blind for three days. Then a disciple named Ananias baptized him, and he could see again. He took the name Paul.\nPaul traveled by ship and on foot across the Mediterranean, preaching to Jews and Gentiles, planting churches and writing letters. He said the Church is one body, with many members. He was put to death in Rome. Paul shows how Christ can change anyone.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a picture of a very large building with four pillars. Say, “Four pillars hold up this building. The Church that Jesus founded stands on four marks too. Let’s find out what they are.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church Jesus founded has four marks, or special signs. We say them in the Nicene Creed on Sundays: ‘I believe in one, holy, catholic and apostolic Church.’ The first mark is that the Church is one. Paul wrote, ‘There is one body and one Spirit… one Lord, one faith, one Baptism, one God and Father of all.’ (Ephesians 4:4-6) We are one because we have one Lord, one faith and one Baptism, and one leader, the Pope.”", "Say: “The second mark is holy. The Church is holy because Jesus is holy and he makes her holy, with the sacraments and his teaching. The members of the Church are sinners, but the Church herself is holy, because Christ is her head. The third mark is catholic, which means ‘universal.’ The Church is for everyone, everywhere, in all times. She has the fullness of the truth and the means of salvation.”", "Say: “The fourth mark is apostolic. The Church is built on the apostles, whom Jesus chose. Their successors, the bishops, continue their work. We trace the line of bishops back to the apostles in an unbroken chain. When you go to Mass anywhere in the world, you will see the same faith, because of these marks.”"],
    ask=["What are the four marks? (One, holy, catholic, apostolic.)", "What does ‘catholic’ mean? (Universal, for everyone everywhere.)", "Why is the Church holy even though her members sin? (Jesus is holy, and gives her holiness.)"],
    practice=["Cut a picture of a church into four pieces labeled One, Holy, Catholic, Apostolic.", "Give each group a set. They put it together and, on the back of each piece, write what the mark means in their own words.", "Come up with a symbol for each mark, like a hand-in-hand circle, a halo, a globe and a chain.", "Share with the class."],
    close="Say the line from the Creed together: “I believe in one, holy, catholic and apostolic Church.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Listen for the four marks in the Creed at Mass.”")),

'3-5-3': dict(
  saint="John Paul II: a pope who loved young people and traveled all over the world.",
  tryit=["Build the Church family tree.", "Find your bishop’s name.", "Say what each member does."],
  saintStory="Karol Wojtyła was born in Poland in 1920. He loved sports, theater and the mountains. As a young man, during World War II, he worked in a quarry and a factory, and he secretly studied for the priesthood when it was forbidden. He became a priest, then a bishop and cardinal.\nIn 1978 he was elected pope and took the name John Paul II. He was the first non-Italian pope in over four hundred years. He traveled to over a hundred countries, kissing the ground when he arrived. He started World Youth Day, inviting young people from all over the world, and he said to them, “Be not afraid!” In 1981 he was shot, and later he visited the man who shot him in prison and forgave him.\nHe died in 2005, and was named a saint in 2014. As Pope he shows how the leader of the Church serves the whole family.",
  script=dict(
    open="Draw a big family tree on the board with names of a family. Ask, “How is this family organized? Who is the grandparent? Who is the parent?” Say, “The Church is a family, too, with different roles.”",
    teach=["Say: “Jesus chose twelve apostles, with Peter as their leader. He said to Peter, ‘You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my Church.’ Peter was the first pope. Pope means ‘father.’ The Pope today is Peter’s successor, the Bishop of Rome. He leads the whole Church, teaches the faith and keeps us united.”", "Say: “The bishops are the successors of the apostles. Each one leads a diocese, a local area. Your bishop is the leader of the Church in your area. Priests and deacons help the bishops, teach and celebrate the sacraments. Priests lead parishes. And all the rest of the baptized people are the laity, from the Greek word for ‘people.’ You are part of the laity!”", "Read 1 Corinthians 12:12-27 in short form. Say: “Paul says that the Church is like a body, with many parts. The eye can’t say to the hand, ‘I don’t need you.’ Each part is important. All of us share Jesus’ mission: to love God and others, and to tell the world about Jesus. Laypeople are teachers, doctors, parents, farmers, and each can bring Jesus to their place.”"],
    ask=["Who is the Pope? What is his job? (The leader of the whole Church, Peter’s successor.)", "Who leads the diocese? (The bishop.) The parish? (The priest.)", "What is the job of the laity? (To bring Jesus to the world: at home, school and work.)"],
    practice=["Draw a chart on a large sheet: Jesus at the top; below him Peter and the apostles; then the Pope; the bishops; priests and deacons; and the laity.", "Find out the name of the current Pope and your local bishop, and add them.", "Write one job beside each level.", "Add your own name under “the laity” and a way you can serve."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, bless our Pope, our bishop and our priests.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for the Pope and our bishop by name.”")),

'3-6-1': dict(
  saint="Bernard of Clairvaux: a monk who loved Mary and called her the Star of the Sea.",
  tryit=["Make the six panels of Mary’s life.", "Say what Mary does in each.", "Pray the Hail Mary."],
  saintStory="Bernard was born in 1090 in France to a noble family. When he was twenty-two, he went to a poor monastery at Cîteaux with thirty of his friends and relatives, and asked to become a monk. A few years later he started a new monastery at Clairvaux, in a wild valley, and became its abbot.\nBernard was a gifted preacher and writer, and kings and popes asked him for advice. He loved Mary deeply. In a well-known sermon he said that when we are tossed about like a boat on the sea of life, we should “look to the star, call on Mary.” People call Mary the “Star of the Sea” from this.\nBernard is called a Doctor of the Church. He shows us how to love Mary: not as a goddess, but as the mother who leads us to Jesus.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a photo of a mother and child. Ask, “What is special about a mom? Mary was the mom of Jesus. Think about how many moments she was there for him, from the beginning to the end.”",
    teach=["Say: “Mary is the Mother of God. This does not mean that she is older than God, or that she is the source of God’s divinity. It means that her Son, Jesus, is God. A mother is the mother of the whole person, not just the body. Mary’s Son is a divine Person. So Mary is truly the Mother of God. The Church taught this at the Council of Ephesus in 431.”", "Read Luke 1:39-56 in summary: “When Mary heard she would be the mother of the Savior, she went quickly to visit her cousin Elizabeth. Elizabeth said, ‘Blessed are you among women. Why has this happened to me, that the mother of my Lord should come to me?’ And Mary sang the Magnificat: ‘My soul proclaims the greatness of the Lord.’ She said yes at the Annunciation, she gave birth to Jesus, she presented him in the Temple, she found him at twelve, she was at Cana, and she stood by the cross.”", "Say: “Jesus gave Mary to us from the cross: ‘Behold your mother.’ She is our spiritual mother. She prays for us and leads us to Jesus. We honor Mary. We do not worship her. We worship only God. We ask her to pray for us, because she is close to God.”"],
    ask=["Why is Mary called the Mother of God? (Because Jesus is God.)", "What did Mary say at the Annunciation? (Yes, let it be done.)", "What do we do when we honor Mary? (Ask her to pray for us, imitate her faith.)"],
    practice=["Fold a long paper into six panels for a frieze.", "Label them: Annunciation, Visitation, Nativity, Finding in the Temple, Cana, the Cross.", "Draw a scene in each and write a sentence about what Mary does.", "Hang the frieze, and walk along it saying the Hail Mary at each panel."],
    close="Pray the Hail Mary together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray a Hail Mary tonight and ask Mary to pray for your family.”")),

'3-6-2': dict(
  saint="Perpetua: a young mother who held on to Jesus, and her friend Felicity.",
  tryit=["Draw three circles: earth, purgatory, heaven.", "Write names in each.", "Pray for someone in each circle."],
  saintStory="Perpetua was a young, well-educated woman in Carthage, North Africa, around the year 203. She had a baby son, and she was a catechumen, learning to become a Christian. The Roman government had made it illegal to become one. She and a few friends were arrested, including Felicity, a slave who was expecting a baby.\nPerpetua’s father begged her to give up her faith for the sake of her child. She said, “Father, do you see this vase? Can it be called by any other name than what it is?” He said no. “Neither can I call myself anything other than what I am: a Christian.” She wrote a diary in prison, one of the earliest writings by a Christian woman. She and Felicity were put to death for their faith, and the Christians of Carthage told their story.\nPerpetua and Felicity are named in the Mass. They are part of the communion of saints, praying for us.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “Who prays for you? Make a list in your mind. Now, here is a surprise: even people you have never met pray for you. Let’s learn how.”",
    teach=["Say: “We believe in the communion of saints. We say it in the Apostles’ Creed. It means that all of God’s people are one family, united in Jesus, whether they are alive on earth or have died. There are three parts of this family. The Church on earth: all of us still on our journey. The Church in purgatory: souls who died in God’s friendship, but are being cleansed before heaven. And the Church in heaven: the saints, who see God face to face.”", "Say: “Death doesn’t end the family. Hebrews 12:1 says, ‘We are surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses.’ We pray for each other. The saints in heaven pray for us. And we can pray for the souls in purgatory, to help them reach heaven. We do this especially on All Souls’ Day, November 2, and at every Mass.”", "Say: “Why do we ask saints to pray for us? For the same reason we ask a friend: ‘Please pray for me.’ The saints are alive in Christ, closer to God than we are, and their prayers are powerful. They are not ‘in between’ us and God. They pray with us. And on All Saints’ Day, November 1, we celebrate all of them.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘communion of saints’ mean? (The whole family of God: on earth, in purgatory, in heaven.)", "How do we help those in purgatory? (By praying for them, and having Masses offered.)", "Why do we ask saints to pray for us? (They are alive in Christ and close to God.)"],
    practice=["Draw three concentric circles on paper and label them earth, purgatory and heaven.", "Write names in each: people you are praying for, people who have died that you want to pray for, and saints you love.", "Say a short prayer for each name, “Lord, grant them peace,” or “Saint ____, pray for us.”", "Keep the sheet as a prayer list."],
    close="Pray the Eternal Rest prayer: “Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord, and let perpetual light shine upon them.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Say the Eternal Rest prayer for someone who has died.”")),

'3-6-3': dict(
  saint="Stephen: the first martyr, who saw heaven open.",
  tryit=["Draw a road to heaven.", "Put signs on it: honest word, help a friend, share, pray.", "Add one turn that leads away."],
  saintStory="Stephen was one of seven men chosen by the apostles to help care for the poor in the early Church in Jerusalem. The Bible says he was “full of faith and the Holy Spirit.” He did great wonders and spoke so wisely that people could not argue with him.\nSome people in the city became angry with him. They took him to the council and brought false witnesses to lie about him. When Stephen spoke, he told the whole story of God’s people, and said that they had not listened to God’s prophets. This made them furious.\nThe Bible says that Stephen, filled with the Holy Spirit, looked up to heaven and saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing at God’s right hand. He said, “I see the heavens opened!” As he was being stoned, he prayed, “Lord Jesus, receive my spirit,” and “Lord, do not hold this sin against them.” Stephen shows what heaven means for us: even in death, God is waiting.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a drawing of a road, with a signpost, going up a hill. Ask, “Where do you think this road leads? What would you hope to find at the end?” Let them guess. “Today we talk about the end of our road.”",
    teach=["Say: “God made us to be with him forever. That is what heaven is: perfect life with God, in joy, forever. Revelation 21:3-4 says: ‘God himself will be with them. He will wipe away every tear from their eyes, and death shall be no more, neither shall there be mourning nor crying nor pain anymore.’ In heaven, there is no suffering. We will see God face to face. We will be with Mary, the angels, the saints and our loved ones. And we will be perfectly happy.”", "Say: “Jesus opened heaven for us by his death and Resurrection. Before him, heaven was closed. Now it is open to all who love him. But we are free. God does not force us. We can choose God or turn away. Our choices matter. Every time we choose to love, we move closer to him.”", "Say: “God is merciful. If we are sorry, he forgives us. We hope to be with him. And we do not know the day we will die, so we try to live every day in his friendship. We pray, we go to Mass and Confession, and we care for others. Heaven isn’t a faraway dream. It begins now, in our relationship with God.”"],
    ask=["What is heaven? (Perfect life with God forever.)", "Who opened heaven for us? (Jesus.)", "How can we get ready for heaven? (Choose good, pray, receive the sacraments, love others.)"],
    practice=["Give each child a large sheet of paper. Say: “Draw a winding road that leads to a bright place at the top, heaven.”", "Along the road, draw signs for good choices: an honest word, helping a friend, sharing, praying.", "Draw one side road that leads away. Label it with a bad choice, and add a sign: “Turn back: God forgives.”", "Show a partner the map and explain it."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, lead us to heaven.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Tell your family how you can help each other get to heaven.”")),

'3-7-1': dict(
  saint="Ambrose: the bishop who taught new Christians about the sacraments and baptized Augustine.",
  tryit=["Visit the seven stations.", "Touch and look at each sign.", "Say what each gives."],
  saintStory="Ambrose was born around the year 339, the son of a high Roman official. He studied law and became governor of northern Italy, based in Milan. When the bishop of Milan died, the people were arguing about who should be the next bishop. Ambrose came to calm them down, and a child (or someone in the crowd) shouted, “Ambrose for bishop!” and everyone agreed.\nHe wasn’t even baptized yet! Within a week he was baptized, ordained and made bishop. He gave his wealth to the poor and spent his time studying the Bible. He preached well, and a young teacher named Augustine came to hear him, and Ambrose baptized him on Easter in 387.\nAmbrose wrote to the newly baptized about the sacraments, explaining how God works through signs, like water, oil, bread and wine. He shows how God uses physical things to give his grace.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a wedding ring. Ask, “What does this mean? It is a sign of a promise. But it is more than a sign: it actually binds two people together.” Say, “Today we learn about special signs that give us something real.”",
    teach=["Say: “A sacrament is an outward sign, given by Jesus, that gives us grace. An outward sign is something we can see, touch, hear or smell, like water, oil, bread or words. Grace is God’s free gift of his own life. Jesus gave the Church seven sacraments. We use ordinary things, and God does something wonderful.”", "Say: “Jesus often used physical things in his miracles. He touched people. He made mud. He took bread. In John 3:5 he said, ‘Unless one is born of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter the kingdom of God.’ Today, Jesus acts through the sacraments. In Mark 6:56, people were healed just by touching the hem of his cloak. We meet Jesus in the sacraments.”", "Say: “The seven sacraments are: Baptism (water), Confirmation (oil called chrism), Eucharist (bread and wine), Reconciliation (words of forgiveness), Anointing of the Sick (oil), Holy Orders (laying on of hands), and Matrimony (exchange of vows). Each one gives grace for a special need. God does the work: the sacrament works because of Christ.”"],
    ask=["What is a sacrament? (An outward sign, given by Christ, that gives grace.)", "What is grace? (God’s free gift of his life and help.)", "Name the seven sacraments. (Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist, Reconciliation, Anointing, Orders, Matrimony.)"],
    practice=["Set up seven stations, each with a real object: a bowl of water, a bottle of scented oil, bread and a cup, a stole, a small jar of oil, a candle or ring, a wedding ring.", "Give children a gallery card for each. They visit, look and touch, and write the sacrament’s name, and what it gives.", "Rotate every four minutes.", "Gather to share one thing each station taught."],
    close="Pray: “Lord Jesus, thank you for the sacraments.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Name the seven sacraments to your family.”")),

'3-7-2': dict(
  saint="Francis Xavier: a missionary who baptized many people in India and Japan.",
  tryit=["Draw a door, a flame and a table.", "Write what each sacrament gives.", "Tell a partner."],
  saintStory="Francis Xavier was born in 1506 in Spain, in a castle. He went to the University of Paris, where he met a student named Ignatius of Loyola, and became one of the first Jesuits.\nHe sailed to India in 1541, a voyage that took over a year. He walked from village to village, ringing a little bell to gather the children. He taught them the Our Father, the Creed and the Commandments, and baptized many people. He worked among the poor, the sick and the pearl divers. Then he traveled to Japan, and set out for China, but he died on an island off the coast in 1552.\nHe is the patron of missionaries. Francis Xavier shows how Baptism brings people into God’s family, and how the sacraments are for everyone.",
  script=dict(
    open="Draw a door on the board. Ask, “What happens when you walk through a door? You go into a new place. Today we will see three sacraments that welcome us into God’s family.”",
    teach=["Say: “The sacraments of initiation are Baptism, Confirmation and the Eucharist. ‘Initiation’ means beginning, entering. These three sacraments bring us into the Church. They are the way we become full members of God’s family. Adults receive all three at the Easter Vigil. Children often receive them over several years.”", "Read Acts 2:38-42 briefly. Say: “On Pentecost, Peter said to the crowd, ‘Repent, and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ for the forgiveness of your sins; and you shall receive the gift of the Holy Spirit.’ About three thousand were baptized and joined the apostles, to share the breaking of bread and prayers.”", "Say: “Baptism is the door. We are washed with water, our sins are forgiven, and we become children of God. Confirmation is the strength. The bishop (or priest) anoints us with oil called chrism and says, ‘Be sealed with the gift of the Holy Spirit.’ We are strengthened to live our faith. The Eucharist is the meal. We receive Jesus’ Body and Blood, to feed our souls. Baptism is once for all, and so is Confirmation. We receive the Eucharist again and again.”"],
    ask=["What are the three sacraments of initiation? (Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist.)", "What does Baptism give us? (New life, forgiveness, becoming God’s children.)", "How is the Eucharist different from the other two? (We receive it over and over.)"],
    practice=["Give each child a paper with three boxes labeled Door, Flame and Table.", "Draw a church door for Baptism, a flame for Confirmation, a table with bread and a cup for the Eucharist.", "Under each picture write a sentence about what it gives.", "Share with a partner, then say together: “Door, strength, meal.”"],
    close="Pray: “Thank you, God, for bringing us into your family.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask your parents about your baptism, and your First Communion plans.”")),

'3-7-3': dict(
  saint="Camillus de Lellis: a soldier turned nurse who started hospitals for the sick.",
  tryit=["Sort the scene cards: healing or service.", "Match each to a sacrament.", "Say what each gives."],
  saintStory="Camillus was born in Italy in 1550. He grew up tall and strong, and became a soldier, and a gambler who lost everything. A sore on his leg that would not heal sent him to the hospital of San Giacomo in Rome, where he worked as a servant. But he was fired for gambling.\nSoon after, when he was twenty-five, he decided to change his life. He returned to the hospital, and began to care for the patients, as a nurse. He saw how poorly the sick were treated, so he started a group of men who would care for the sick as a mother cares for her child. Their uniform was a black habit with a red cross on the chest. It is the origin of the Red Cross symbol.\nCamillus also became a priest. He is the patron saint of nurses and the sick. He shows how Anointing of the Sick, and love for the sick, bring Jesus’ healing.",
  script=dict(
    open="Place a bandage, a ring and a stole on a table. Ask, “What do these three things have in common? They all show how God cares for us at special times in our lives.”",
    teach=["Say: “There are two sacraments of healing. Reconciliation, also called Confession or Penance, heals our soul when we have sinned. We tell our sins to a priest, we say we are sorry, and the priest forgives us in the name of Jesus. Anointing of the Sick heals us when we are seriously ill, or old. The priest anoints the forehead and hands with oil and prays. It gives strength, peace and courage, forgives sins, and sometimes brings healing.”", "Read James 5:14-15: “Is anyone among you sick? Let him call for the elders of the church, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord. And the prayer of faith will save the sick man, and the Lord will raise him up.” Say: “Anointing of the Sick is for anyone who is in danger because of sickness or old age. It is not only for people who are dying.”", "Say: “There are also two sacraments of service. Holy Orders: Jesus calls some men to be bishops, priests and deacons, to serve God’s people, teach, and celebrate the sacraments. Matrimony: a man and a woman promise to love each other for life, and God gives them the grace to be faithful. They serve each other and their children. All the sacraments are for others, but these two are especially for helping others get to heaven.”"],
    ask=["What are the two sacraments of healing? (Reconciliation and Anointing of the Sick.)", "What are the two sacraments of service? (Holy Orders and Matrimony.)", "What does the priest use in the Anointing of the Sick? (Blessed oil.)"],
    practice=["Prepare scene cards: a grandfather in the hospital; a child who told a lie; a wedding; a young man being ordained.", "Make two headings: “Healing” and “Service.”", "Children sort the cards, then name the sacrament for each and say what it gives.", "Choose one scene and draw or act it out."],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, heal us and help us serve.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for someone who is sick.”")),

'3-8-1': dict(
  saint="Ephrem the Syrian: a deacon who wrote hymns and prayers that are still used.",
  tryit=["Draw your day.", "Write a short prayer for each part.", "Choose one to pray each day."],
  saintStory="Ephrem was born around the year 306 in the city of Nisibis, in what is now Turkey. He became a deacon, a helper to the bishop, and a teacher. He wrote many poems, hymns and homilies in his own language, Syriac, to teach the faith and to praise God.\nWhen a war forced him to leave his home, he settled in the city of Edessa and kept writing. He taught that creation is full of signs of God, like a book. He helped the poor, and during a famine he organized relief. People loved to sing his hymns, even when some false teachers copied the melodies, so he wrote new, true words.\nEphrem is called the Harp of the Holy Spirit. He wrote a prayer that Christians still pray in Lent: “O Lord and Master of my life, take from me the spirit of sloth.” He shows us that prayer can be made of words and songs.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “Pretend you are on the phone with your best friend. What do you do? You talk, you listen, you share news. Prayer is a conversation like that with God.” Let the children share about phone or video calls.",
    teach=["Say: “Prayer means raising our hearts and minds to God. We talk to him, and we listen. God invites us to pray, and he always hears us. Jesus’ own friends asked, ‘Lord, teach us to pray,’ and he taught them the Our Father. (Luke 11:1-4) Prayer is not just words. It is spending time with someone who loves us.”", "Say: “We can pray anywhere, anytime: in a church, in a car, in bed, at the beach. We can pray with words, with songs or in silence. We can use prayers we know by heart, or our own words. God is always listening. Why should we pray? Because we are made for God, and prayer is how we grow closer. Without it, any friendship dies.”", "Say: “Prayer is not just asking for things. It is also thanking, praising, saying sorry and listening. And prayer works. Not always as we expect, but God answers with what is best for us. A good habit is to pray at set times, like morning and bedtime, and before meals. The Church also gives us prayers like the Morning Offering, the Our Father and the Hail Mary.”"],
    ask=["What is prayer? (Raising our hearts and minds to God.)", "When can we pray? (Always, anywhere.)", "What are some good times to pray? (Morning, meals, bedtime, when worried.)"],
    practice=["Give each child a paper divided into four sections: morning, meals, school, bedtime.", "In each section, draw a picture, and write a short prayer: “Morning Offering,” “Bless us, O Lord,” “Come, Holy Spirit,” “Angel of God.”", "Take turns reading a prayer to a partner.", "Choose one to start doing this week."],
    close="Pray the Sign of the Cross, and the Morning Offering: “O Jesus, through the Immaculate Heart of Mary, I offer you my prayers, works, joys and sufferings of this day.” Send home: “Use your prayer chart this week.”")),

'3-8-2': dict(
  saint="Hildegard of Bingen: an abbess who praised God in music.",
  tryit=["Visit each of the five prayer corners.", "Pray in the way shown.", "Write a prayer for each."],
  saintStory="Hildegard was born in 1098 in Germany, the tenth child of a noble family. When she was eight years old, her parents offered her to the Church, and she went to live with a holy woman next to a monastery. She grew up to be an abbess, and led a community of nuns.\nHildegard had visions of God, which she wrote down, and she also wrote about plants and medicine, and composed over seventy songs and a musical drama, in beautiful, soaring melodies. She wrote letters to popes, emperors and kings, and traveled to preach. She told people to praise God with music and to take care of the earth.\nHildegard is a Doctor of the Church. She shows how praise can be prayer, and how we can use gifts like music to worship.",
  script=dict(
    open="Play a minute of beautiful music, such as a chant or a song of praise. Say, “How does it make you feel? Music can lift our hearts to God. It is one way to pray.”",
    teach=["Read Psalm 150 aloud. Say: “‘Praise the Lord! Praise God in his sanctuary… Praise him with trumpet sound, praise him with lute and harp… Let everything that breathes praise the Lord!’ The Psalms are the prayer book of God’s people. Jesus prayed them. They teach us different ways to pray.”", "Say: “The Catechism names five forms of prayer. Blessing and adoration: we bless God, who blesses us, and we adore him as our Creator. Petition: we ask God for what we need, and sorry for our sins. Intercession: we pray for others. Thanksgiving: we thank God for everything. Praise: we praise God just because he is God. Many prayers include more than one.”", "Say: “There are also three ways to express prayer. Vocal prayer uses words, aloud or in our minds, like the Our Father. Meditation is thinking about God’s word, such as imagining a Gospel scene and asking, ‘What is Jesus saying to me?’ Contemplation is resting in God’s love, quietly, without many words, like sitting with someone you love. All three are good, and we can use them all.”"],
    ask=["Name the five forms of prayer. (Blessing/adoration, petition, intercession, thanksgiving, praise.)", "What is vocal prayer? (Prayer in words.)", "How is meditation different from contemplation? (Meditation thinks about God’s word; contemplation rests in his love.)"],
    practice=["Set up five corners, each with a card: Bless/Adore (kneel and be quiet), Petition (write a request), Intercession (write a name of someone to pray for), Thanksgiving (write three thank-yous), Praise (sing or say, “Alleluia!”).", "Groups spend about four minutes at each corner.", "At each, they pray in that way.", "Gather and say one prayer from each corner aloud."],
    close="Pray the Our Father together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Try a different kind of prayer each day.”")),

'3-8-3': dict(
  saint="Louis de Montfort: a priest who taught people to pray the Rosary.",
  tryit=["Make a one-decade rosary.", "Learn how to pray a decade.", "Think about a mystery."],
  saintStory="Louis-Marie Grignion was born in France in 1673. He became a priest and spent his life traveling around western France, giving missions, which are weeks of preaching, in country parishes. He often walked, and slept in poor places.\nLouis loved Mary, and he taught people to pray the Rosary. He gave out rosaries, led processions, and wrote a book called True Devotion to Mary. He taught that devotion to Mary leads us to Jesus. Some people didn’t like his message and he was sent away several times. He kept going. He died at forty-three, worn out from work.\nLouis de Montfort shows how the Rosary can be a family prayer, for everyone, anywhere.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a rosary and say, “What is this? What do people do with it?” Let children share. “It looks like jewelry, but it is a tool for prayer. It is like a spiritual road map.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Rosary is a prayer with Mary. We say the Our Father, ten Hail Marys and the Glory Be, while thinking about moments in the life of Jesus and Mary, called mysteries. A set of ten Hail Marys is called a decade. A full Rosary has five decades. We use beads to keep count, so we can focus on the mysteries.”", "Say: “There are twenty mysteries, in four sets. The Joyful Mysteries: the Annunciation, the Visitation, the Nativity, the Presentation and the Finding in the Temple. The Luminous Mysteries (the mysteries of Light): the Baptism of Jesus, the Wedding at Cana, the Proclamation of the Kingdom, the Transfiguration and the Institution of the Eucharist. The Sorrowful Mysteries: the Agony in the Garden, the Scourging, the Crowning with Thorns, the Carrying of the Cross and the Crucifixion. The Glorious Mysteries: the Resurrection, the Ascension, the Descent of the Holy Spirit, the Assumption and the Coronation of Mary.”", "Say: “Why do we pray it? When we say the words of the Hail Mary, our minds can rest on the story of Jesus. It is like looking at a photo album with Mary, who knows the story best. The Rosary is for everyone: it is simple enough for a child and deep enough for a saint. Many families pray it together.”"],
    ask=["What are the four sets of mysteries? (Joyful, Luminous, Sorrowful, Glorious.)", "What is a decade? (Ten Hail Marys.)", "Why do we use beads? (To count, so we can focus on the mystery.)"],
    practice=["Give each child ten beads, a short string or pipe cleaner, and a small cross.", "Help them string the beads and tie knots, and attach the cross.", "Show how to hold the rosary and pray one decade, starting with the Our Father, then ten Hail Marys, and the Glory Be.", "Choose a mystery, such as the Nativity, and think about it silently while praying one decade together."],
    close="Pray a decade together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray a decade with someone at home.”")),

'3-9-1': dict(
  saint="Ignatius of Antioch: a bishop who wrote that Christians live by the Lord’s Day.",
  tryit=["Design a Sunday poster.", "Put Mass, rest, family and service in the four boxes.", "Plan how to make Sunday special."],
  saintStory="Ignatius was the bishop of Antioch, in Syria, a city where followers of Jesus were first called Christians. He lived about the year 100, and tradition says that he had known the apostle John.\nWhen Ignatius was an old man, the Roman emperor ordered him arrested and taken to Rome to be killed in the arena. As he traveled, under guard, he wrote seven letters to churches along the way. He told the Christians to stay united with their bishop, to celebrate the Eucharist, and to love one another. He wrote that Christians no longer keep the Sabbath, but live by the Lord’s Day, when our life rose through Jesus.\nHe wrote to the Romans, begging them not to save him from martyrdom. Ignatius shows how early Christians loved Sunday, the Eucharist and the Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a calendar with seven days. Ask, “Which day of the week is the most special? What do you do?” Let them share. “We’ll see how God made one day of the week extra special, and why.”",
    teach=["Say: “Sunday is called the Lord’s Day. On Sunday morning, Jesus rose from the dead. That is why we gather on Sunday. The Psalm says, ‘This is the day which the Lord has made; let us rejoice and be glad in it.’ (Psalm 118:24) It is the first day of the week, and in a way it is the eighth day, a day for new creation.”", "Say: “The third commandment says to keep holy the Sabbath. Christians do this on Sunday. On Sunday, Catholics are required to go to Mass, to hear God’s word, receive Jesus in Holy Communion, and give thanks together. Missing Mass without a serious reason, such as illness, is a serious matter, because we are neglecting our relationship with God and his family. We also rest from unnecessary work, and spend time with family.”", "Say: “Sunday is a gift, not a burden. God knows that we need rest, joy and each other. The Mass is the high point of the week. And our week begins and ends around it. It’s also a good day for visiting grandparents, playing games together, going out in nature and helping others.”"],
    ask=["Why do Catholics go to Mass on Sunday? (It is the Lord’s Day, Jesus’ Resurrection.)", "What do we do at Mass? (Hear God’s word, receive Jesus, give thanks.)", "How can we make Sunday special? (Mass, rest, family, service.)"],
    practice=["Give each child a paper divided into four boxes: Mass, Rest, Family, Service.", "Brainstorm what could go in each: wearing nice clothes, singing; a nap, a walk; a family meal, a game; visiting a neighbor, helping at the parish.", "Each child writes or draws ideas in each box.", "Take the poster home and ask the family to try it."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, thank you for Sunday.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Make a plan for next Sunday.”")),

'3-9-2': dict(
  saint="Gregory the Great: a pope who shaped the Church’s prayer and music.",
  tryit=["Color the church year wheel.", "Use the right colors.", "Say what each season means."],
  saintStory="Gregory was born in Rome around 540, to a rich family. He became the prefect, the chief official of the city. But after his father died, he turned his family home into a monastery and gave away his wealth.\nIn 590, when Rome was suffering from floods, plague and war, Gregory was chosen as pope. He didn’t want the job. He called himself “servant of the servants of God.” He fed the poor of the city, organized the Church’s lands, wrote books, and sent a group of monks to England to preach. He also is remembered for the chant, known as “Gregorian chant,” a simple, beautiful way to sing the Church’s prayers.\nGregory is called Gregory the Great. He shows how the liturgy, the Church’s worship, can be beautiful and orderly.",
  script=dict(
    open="Put four items on a table: a purple cloth, a white cloth, a green cloth, a red cloth. Say, “These are the colors the priest wears. Have you noticed that they change? They tell a story. Let’s learn it.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church’s year is not like the calendar year. It begins with Advent, in late November or early December. It tells the whole story of Jesus every year. Here are the seasons. Advent is four weeks of waiting for Jesus, and the color is purple. Christmas celebrates his birth, and the color is white. Ordinary Time is the time between, when we listen to Jesus’ teachings and grow, and the color is green. (‘Ordinary’ comes from ‘ordinal,’ meaning ‘numbered,’ the weeks are counted.)”", "Say: “Lent is forty days before Easter. We pray, fast and give to the poor. The color is purple again. The Easter Triduum, the three days from Holy Thursday to Easter, is the holiest time. Then comes Easter, which is fifty days long, ending with Pentecost. The color is white, and on Pentecost it is red, for the fire of the Holy Spirit. Red is also used for martyrs. Isaiah 40:1-5 says: ‘Comfort, comfort my people… prepare the way of the Lord.’ That is Advent’s message.”", "Say: “Why do we have seasons? Because we need time to remember and celebrate each part of Jesus’ life. They help us live the mysteries of our faith, not just hear about them. Just as nature has seasons, so does the Church. And the colors help us see it.”"],
    ask=["What color is Advent? (Purple.)", "What is the longest season? (Ordinary Time.)", "What does red mean? (The Holy Spirit, and martyrs.)"],
    practice=["Give each child a paper plate or a circle.", "Divide it into sections: Advent, Christmas, Ordinary Time, Lent, Triduum and Easter, Pentecost.", "Color each section the right color and write the name and one thing we do in that season.", "Share the wheels, and hang them in the room."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, help us walk with you all year.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Notice the color the priest wears next Sunday.”")),

'3-9-3': dict(
  saint="Frances Xavier Cabrini: an immigrant who cared for other immigrants.",
  tryit=["Pick a saint.", "Make a saint card: name, feast day, a virtue and a prayer.", "Share it."],
  saintStory="Frances Cabrini was born in Italy in 1850, the youngest of thirteen children. She was small and often sick, but she wanted to be a missionary to China. She started a community of sisters, the Missionary Sisters of the Sacred Heart.\nThe Pope sent her not to China, but to the United States, to help the many Italian immigrants arriving in New York. They were poor, often sick, and discriminated against. In 1889 she arrived with six sisters, and together they started schools, orphanages and hospitals in New York, Chicago, New Orleans, Seattle and many other cities. She crossed the ocean about thirty times.\nShe died in 1917, and was named the first American citizen to be a saint, in 1946. She is the patron of immigrants. She shows how an ordinary person with God’s help can do great things.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a calendar with several feast days marked. Ask, “Which of these days do you know? Christmas, Easter… Did you know that almost every day of the year is the feast of a saint?” Show examples: Nicholas, Patrick, Francis.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church celebrates special days besides Sunday. Some of them are feasts of Mary, like the Immaculate Conception on December 8, the Annunciation on March 25, and the Assumption on August 15. Others are feasts of saints, such as Saint Nicholas on December 6, and Saint Patrick on March 17. The Church remembers a saint on the day they died, which is their birthday into heaven.”", "Say: “Some days are called holy days of obligation. These are special days besides Sunday when Catholics are required to go to Mass. In the United States they include Christmas (December 25), Mary, Mother of God (January 1), the Ascension, the Assumption (August 15), All Saints (November 1) and the Immaculate Conception (December 8). Check your own diocese, as some rules vary.”", "Read Revelation 7:9-17 in summary. Say: “John saw a huge crowd from every nation, wearing white robes, praising God. They are the saints. The feasts of saints remind us that ordinary people, with God’s help, can become holy. A saint is not a perfect person. A saint is someone who kept trying, even after falling. And you can be a saint too.”"],
    ask=["What is a feast day? (A day to remember a saint, or an event in Jesus’ or Mary’s life.)", "What is a holy day of obligation? (A special day when Catholics go to Mass.)", "Who is your favorite saint, and why? (Take answers.)"],
    practice=["Give each child an index card, and a list of saints to choose from, or have children choose a saint by their name day, a patron of something they like, or a hero.", "On the card, write: the saint’s name, the feast day, a virtue they showed, and a short prayer.", "Add a small drawing.", "Share cards in small groups. Collect them into a class “saints album.”"],
    close="Pray: “Saints of God, pray for us.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look up your saint’s feast day and celebrate it.”")),
}
